00:07:33,581 --> 00:07:37,660
In one year, we make 180 million cookies.

138
00:07:37,661 --> 00:07:40,060
It's going to take a much bigger glass of milk than I brought.

139
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:46,560
Entenmann's chocolate chip cookie is the company's best-selling cookie.

140
00:07:46,561 --> 00:07:53,160
And since its debut in 1974, 161 million boxes have been sold.

141
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:58,540
161 million boxes weigh nearly the same as four Brooklyn Bridges.

142
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:05,220
Those kind of huge numbers can only be produced because of the modern tech of the 21st century.

143
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:08,720
But now, let's go a little further back in time.

144
00:08:08,721 --> 00:08:15,400
way back when the magical cookie was just the after effect of another basic kitchen task.

145
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:19,660
You can date cookies themselves back to 7th century Persia.

146
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:25,660
Back then, the actual temperature of an oven was impossible to measure,

147
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:30,180
and oven thermostats weren't even introduced until towards the end of World War II.

148
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,300
So when 7th century Persians would bake cakes,

149
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:37,020
they would test a small amount of batter to test the oven temperature.

150
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:41,860
Culinary historians say these test cakes were the very first cookies.

151
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,960
As people migrated throughout Europe, cookies moved with them.

152
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:50,460
There's something ephemeral about a cookie that gives you just pure joy.

153
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:55,700
The fact that you can actually grab a cookie with your hands, that you don't need utensils,

154
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:59,980
there's also so much creativity in what you can put into a cookie.

155
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:01,340
They're magical.

156
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:06,440
The following centuries led to an increased demand for cookies,

157
00:09:06,441 --> 00:09:17,157
And in the 19th century, new kinds of cookies were introduced, including commercially made ones like vanilla wafers, animal crackers, and a surprisingly square cookie,

158
00:09:17,158 --> 00:09:18,120
the Fig Newton.

159
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:27,080
The Fig Newton was introduced as a brand in 1891, and it really at the time played off this big move for healthy foods.

160
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:31,840
The idea of figs being full of fiber, the grains being full of nutrients and minerals.

161
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:37,120
It was really pitched as something that was really better for you than like a sweet, delicious snack.

162
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:43,400
By the way, Newton is not after Sir Isaac Newton, which I always thought was the case.

163
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:49,080
It's named after Newton, Massachusetts, which is pretty close to where the factory was.

164
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:54,260
Later in 1898, the cookie craze took off on a national scale

165
00:09:54,261 --> 00:09:58,900
when several American bakeries merged to form the National Biscuit Company,

166
00:09:58,901 --> 00:10:01,960
The company that would later become Nabisco.

167
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:09,940
The cookie geniuses that would eventually introduce cookie favorites like Oreos, Teddy Grahams, and Malomars.

168
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:15,740
It just shows you our passion for doing something no one's ever done before.

169
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:21,160
And that spirit of American innovation can be found in that little disc of sugar and fat.

170
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:25,840
Here at Entenmann's, 1,200 pounds of cookie dough

171
00:10:25,841 --> 00:10:27,480
