was discovered by accident?

259
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:10,300
The story of how chocolate chip cookies were invented

260
00:14:10,301 --> 00:14:13,440
is a mix of history and myth.

261
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:14,860
Here's what we do now.

262
00:14:14,861 --> 00:14:19,040
Ruth Wakefield invented chocolate chip cookies in the 1930s.

263
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:23,800
Wakefield owned a popular restaurant in Massachusetts called Toll House.

264
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:26,700
Well, that name sounds familiar.

265
00:14:27,580 --> 00:14:30,200
Back then, it was just the Toll House Inn.

266
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:35,120
And one day, Wakefield decided to whip up a batch of her butter drop cookies.

267
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:38,520
How exactly did Wakefield invent the chocolate chip cookie?

268
00:14:38,900 --> 00:14:40,120
There are a lot of stories.

269
00:14:40,121 --> 00:14:43,360
the most popular one is probably that one day she realized

270
00:14:43,361 --> 00:14:44,960
that she was out of Baker's chocolate.

271
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,860
Instead of running to the store or dumping the batch in the trash,

272
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,040
she decided to chop up a bar of semi-sweet chocolate.

273
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:54,600
But it wasn't just any chocolate.

274
00:14:55,060 --> 00:14:56,400
It was Nestle's.

275
00:14:56,640 --> 00:15:00,140
Wakefield dropped the chopped up Nestle bar into the brown sugar dough,

276
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:03,440
expecting the chocolate to melt and disperse throughout the cookie

277
00:15:03,441 --> 00:15:04,980
during the baking process.

278
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:09,380
Instead, the chocolate pieces melted

279
00:15:09,381 --> 00:15:13,940
while retaining their form, becoming pockets of gooey chocolate.

280
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:17,340
And what came out was the world's first chocolate chip cookie.

281
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:20,900
But it wasn't until three years after this happy accident

282
00:15:20,901 --> 00:15:25,720
that Nestle started selling their now-famous chocolate morsels in 1940.

283
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:29,520
Not-too-thick, not-too-thin cookie.

284
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:32,960
The outsides are crisp and crunchy,

285
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:36,200
and there's, like, some toasty notes, some caramelization,

286
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:38,620
a few bits of chocolate here and there.

287
00:15:38,621 --> 00:15:42,560
In the middle, it's nice and soft and gooey and melty.

288
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:45,160
I need a glass of milk now.

289
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:50,020
To this day, Ruth Wakefield's original chocolate chip cookie recipe

290
00:15:50,021 --> 00:15:54,420
is printed on the back of Nestle Toll House's bags of chocolate chips.

291
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:56,700
I love a good happy accident.

292
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:03,020
And thinking about Ruth Wakefield messing around with some new ideas

293
00:16:03,021 --> 00:16:05,420
gives me an idea of my own.

294
00:16:05,421 --> 00:16:10,420
So what I was thinking is, if I take some cookie dough, right?

295
00:16:10,421 --> 00:16:13,420
And then I was thinking, take one of these beautiful strawberries

296
00:16:13,421 --> 00:16:18,420
and then wrap it in the cookie dough and then dip it,

297
00:16:18,421 --> 00:16:22,420
because you guys have those amazing donuts and cookies that have the chocolate,

298
00:16:25,420 --> 00:16:27,420
and let it set in the fridge.

299
00:16:27,421 --> 00:16:31,420
Let's see if we just made a next-level treat.

300
00:16:31,421 --> 00:16:34,420
We're just going to hang out and eat cookie dough until they're ready.

301
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:39,420
All right, let's see if this guy has had a chance to set up.

302
00:16:39,421 --> 00:16:41,420
And look at these.

303
00:16:41,421 --> 00:16:44,420
Sometimes the noodle works.

304
00:16:44,421 --> 00:16:50,420
I have one pretty one, and I will not give someone who has been as gracious a host something subpar.

305
00:16:50,421 --> 00:16:53,420
So let me give you this pretty one. Let me know what you think.

306
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:03,420
Wow, this is awesome. Really, really good.

307
00:17:03,421 --> 00:17:04,880
It's so good.

308
00:17:04,881 --> 00:17:07,080
I just think that the crunchy hard shell,

309
00:17:07,081 --> 00:17:10,000
the soft pillowy cookie dough,

310
00:17:10,001 --> 00:17:11,720
and then like that lush strawberry.

311
00:17:11,721 --> 00:17:13,760
It's like three different textures.

312
00:17:13,761 --> 00:17:15,940
They say good ingredients makes good product.

313
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:18,900
Never more true than in this.

314
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:21,600
Marvelous.

315
00:17:21,601 --> 00:17:24,240
I wish you could all try this delicious creation

316
00:17:24,241 --> 00:17:28,320
for yourselves, but since Entenmann's doesn't sell cookie dough,

317
00:17:28,321 --> 00:17:30,940
I thought I'd let you in on some secrets of a company

318
00:17:30,941 --> 00:17:33,200
that sells a lot of cookie dough.

319
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:42,960
While Otis Spunkmeyer produces their own brand of cookies,

320
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:45,580
they also make frozen cookie dough pucks

321
00:17:45,581 --> 00:17:49,540
for a closely guarded list of the country's leading fast food chains,

322
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:55,060
serving Otis cookies at upwards of 54,000 restaurant locations

323
00:17:55,061 --> 00:17:56,380
across the states.

324
00:17:57,140 --> 00:18:00,660
Even the U.S. Army gets their cookies from Otis.

325
00:18:00,940 --> 00:18:03,940
Mark Reisinger is the bakery director

326
00:18:03,941 --> 00:18:07,940
who leads the team here at Otis' Chaska, Minnesota facility.

327
00:18:07,941 --> 00:18:11,940
We oversee operations of about a 100,000-square-foot building,

328
00:18:11,941 --> 00:18:13,940
and we have three production lines.

329
00:18:13,941 --> 00:18:16,940
This isn't your bag of chocolate chip cookies

330
00:18:16,941 --> 00:18:19,940
or your little two-pound bag of flour.

331
00:18:19,941 --> 00:18:20,940
When flour comes into my facility,

332
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:23,940
it's coming in in 50,000-pound truckloads.

333
00:18:23,941 --> 00:18:26,940
Today, Mark's team is making frozen cookie dough pucks,

334
00:18:26,941 --> 00:18:29,940
specifically their chocolate chunk cookie.

335
00:18:29,941 --> 00:18:33,280
Many larger chain restaurants don't make their own cookie dough,

336
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:36,420
so they contract companies like Otis to do the groundwork,

337
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:38,440
freezing the dough to lock in the freshness

338
00:18:38,540 --> 00:18:40,880
so that restaurants can bake cookies fresh

339
00:18:40,980 --> 00:18:42,860
at their individual locations.

340
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:45,280
So it's just like a recipe that you would make at home.

341
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:47,920
It's just instead of making a bowl of cookies,

342
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:50,660
I'm making it in 3,000-pound quantities.

343
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:53,560
And to make 3,000 pounds of cookie dough,

344
00:18:53,660 --> 00:18:57,640
Mark's team uses giant scales and weighs out ingredients,

345
00:18:57,641 --> 00:19:00,940
like nearly 50 pounds of molasses and vanilla,

346
00:19:00,941 --> 00:19:05,780
450 pounds of butter, 200 pounds of liquid eggs,

347
00:19:05,781 --> 00:19:09,480
and 600 pounds of chocolate chunks.

348
00:19:11,980 --> 00:19:14,900
Now that all the hundreds of pounds of ingredients

349
00:19:14,901 --> 00:19:18,200
have been added to the massive mechanical mixer,

350
00:19:18,201 --> 00:19:21,200
it's time to start portioning out the dough.

351
00:19:21,201 --> 00:19:24,540
It moves down the line, and it gets sheeted out,

352
00:19:24,541 --> 00:19:28,040
down through one kind of consistent layer

353
00:19:28,041 --> 00:19:30,880
so that the next machine, which is the wire cut machine,

354
00:19:30,881 --> 00:19:33,540
it can handle it and it gets a consistent weight.

355
00:19:33,541 --> 00:19:36,120
And that's how we control the weight of that cookie

356
00:19:36,121 --> 00:19:39,580
so I can get that exact weight that they would like every time.

357
00:19:39,581 --> 00:19:41,620
Now that Jose has checked the weights

358
00:19:41,621 --> 00:19:44,120
and made sure it meets customer specifications,

359
00:19:44,121 --> 00:19:47,300
the cookie dough pucks will march down the line

360
00:19:47,301 --> 00:19:49,200
and they will go into the freezer.

361
00:19:49,201 --> 00:19:52,200
From here, the fresh cookie dough is frozen

362
00:19:52,201 --> 00:19:55,200
at a temperature of negative 30 degrees.

363
00:19:55,201 --> 00:19:57,500
The frozen cookie pucks are coming out to discharge,

364
00:19:57,501 --> 00:20:01,500
and they go up a series of conveyors to a scaling system.

365
00:20:01,501 --> 00:20:05,120
At the scaling system, there's multiple heads on it,

366
00:20:05,121 --> 00:20:08,180
and the computer system knows this head and this head

367
00:20:08,181 --> 00:20:11,320
and this head have exactly 27 pucks into them,

368
00:20:11,321 --> 00:20:13,120
which is what the customer requests,

369
00:20:13,121 --> 00:20:16,360
and it drops it down into my bagging and sealing process.

370
00:20:16,361 --> 00:20:20,000
They're packaged frozen in order to retain their consistency

371
00:20:20,001 --> 00:20:23,600
and so that restaurants can bake them fresh to serve to their customers.

372
00:20:27,540 --> 00:20:30,840
As the product's being packed, we actually take a sample off the line.

373
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:33,660
So they're going to grab a bag that could have gone to a customer,

374
00:20:33,820 --> 00:20:36,280
and they're going to take it to what we call our quality lab.

375
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:41,660
Since each of Otis' customers order different cookies with different specs,

376
00:20:42,140 --> 00:20:45,900
Otis customizes every detail for their clients' needs.

377
00:20:45,901 --> 00:20:48,520
And to consistently deliver on these promises,

378
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:50,900
Otis leaves no detail unchecked.

379
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:53,960
And they're going to bake it off in the exact same oven

380
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:57,020
that the customer would use for the exact same amount of time.

381
00:20:57,400 --> 00:20:59,240
Once the cookies are pulled out of the oven,

382
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:01,920
we track all of the specifications

383
00:21:01,921 --> 00:21:05,660
to make sure that it is within the customer range

384
00:21:05,661 --> 00:21:08,000
so that we're giving them the same quality cookie

385
00:21:08,001 --> 00:21:09,340
every batch we make.

386
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:11,040
We taste every batch that we bake

387
00:21:11,041 --> 00:21:12,920
to make sure it's all good to go.

388
00:21:13,140 --> 00:21:13,980
Do you get to taste them?

389
00:21:14,180 --> 00:21:14,880
Yes, we do.

390
00:21:14,881 --> 00:21:15,581
Oh, you do?

391
00:21:15,582 --> 00:21:17,000
You do it and you spit it out.

392
00:21:17,001 --> 00:21:18,380
Oh, you spit it out? Why?

393
00:21:18,381 --> 00:21:20,140
You can't eat the whole cookie all day long.

394
00:21:20,141 --> 00:21:21,640
Like this.

395
00:21:21,641 --> 00:21:25,980
Nearly 25,000 cookie pucks will be made today

396
00:21:25,981 --> 00:21:28,980
and shipped to restaurants across the country.

397
00:21:28,981 --> 00:21:31,660
And that's just to one restaurant chain.

398
00:21:31,661 --> 00:21:35,560
This week, Otis Bunkmeyer will be switching up the formulas

399
00:21:35,561 --> 00:21:38,200
to make a number of frozen cookie dough varieties

400
00:21:38,201 --> 00:21:41,040
to ship to other retailers across the states.

401
00:21:41,041 --> 00:21:44,900
But while Otis Bunkmeyer makes several cookie varieties,

402
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:48,300
there's one iconic kind they don't make.

403
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:52,440
It's a sandwich cookie that's wonder-filled

404
00:21:52,441 --> 00:21:55,560
with a slew of imaginative flavors inside.

405
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:58,420
How could we ever celebrate cookies

406
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:01,800
without the one, the only Oreo?

407
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:06,220
One of the things I have to say about Oreos that I really enjoy

408
00:22:06,221 --> 00:22:08,700
is that they're really good at producing wild flavors.

409
00:22:09,060 --> 00:22:10,260
Sometimes you have to go to different countries

410
00:22:10,261 --> 00:22:11,380
to actually find these.

411
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:13,140
So I happened to be in Bangkok

412
00:22:13,141 --> 00:22:15,240
where I discovered tiramisu-flavored Oreos,

413
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:17,120
and nothing can go back ever since.

414
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:21,740
Yet surprisingly, they're not the original cookie sandwich.

415
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:25,840
Four years before Oreos were introduced in 1912,

416
00:22:26,180 --> 00:22:29,620
there was already a sandwich cookie called Hydrox,

417
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:31,940
which debuted in 1908.

418
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:36,520
But even though Oreo is the number one selling cookie on the planet today,

419
00:22:36,521 --> 00:22:40,200
it took nearly four decades before Oreo sales

420
00:22:40,201 --> 00:22:44,140
overtook the Hydrox cookie in the mid-1950s.

421
00:22:45,400 --> 00:22:49,460
The Oreo versus Hydrox rivalry continues to this day,

422
00:22:49,620 --> 00:22:51,840
but no matter which your favorite is,

423
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:54,680
it's time to adore the marvelous creation

424
00:22:54,681 --> 00:22:56,480
we've been crafting today,

425
00:22:57,060 --> 00:23:00,540
the Entenmann Soft-Baked Chocolate Chip Cookie.

426
00:23:01,020 --> 00:23:03,660
If there's a prettier sight, I do not know it.

427
00:23:04,460 --> 00:23:08,660
How do you guys resist the urge to, like, just, like...

428
00:23:08,661 --> 00:23:10,320
Well, they're too hot.

429
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:14,460
Where do you say the perfect place is to try the cookie?

430
00:23:14,580 --> 00:23:15,560
In your opinion, John?

431
00:23:15,720 --> 00:23:17,160
In between the two coolers.

432
00:23:17,340 --> 00:23:18,740
I would have to agree with that.

433
00:23:18,840 --> 00:23:20,700
You want them where they're fresh out of the oven

434
00:23:20,701 --> 00:23:23,180
with that crispiness and the chewiness inside.

435
00:23:24,520 --> 00:23:25,580
Where are the coolers?

436
00:23:27,880 --> 00:23:30,460
Let's escape to a place with a different vibe

437
00:23:30,461 --> 00:23:32,880
to take a look at an iconic cookie

438
00:23:32,881 --> 00:23:35,720
that has an inedible surprise inside.

439
00:23:35,721 --> 00:23:42,620
And often, these cookies make us think of a far-off land.

440
00:23:42,621 --> 00:23:46,400
But in actuality, this cookie was born right here in America.

441
00:23:57,200 --> 00:24:00,300
The fortune cookie is the expected dessert

442
00:24:00,301 --> 00:24:02,040
at most Chinese restaurants.

443
00:24:02,260 --> 00:24:05,620
But the origins of these treats with messages inside

444
00:24:05,621 --> 00:24:07,340
aren't Chinese at all.

445
00:24:07,700 --> 00:24:08,440
That's right.

446
00:24:08,920 --> 00:24:12,420
Fortune cookies first appeared on the scene in California

447
00:24:12,421 --> 00:24:14,260
in the early 1900s.

448
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:18,020
And the family-run Oakland Fortune Cookie Factory

449
00:24:18,021 --> 00:24:21,020
has been using their original 1950s equipment

450
00:24:21,021 --> 00:24:24,180
to produce this classic American treat.

451
00:24:24,860 --> 00:24:25,940
My name's Alicia.

452
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:28,660
I run the Oakland Fortune Cookie Factory.

453
00:24:29,140 --> 00:24:33,600
Currently, I am the fourth generation to help run the factory.

454
00:24:35,620 --> 00:24:40,180
The company was originally founded in 1957 by Calvin Wong.

455
00:24:40,820 --> 00:24:42,400
When the factory first opened,

456
00:24:42,620 --> 00:24:45,540
they were using some of the newest modern technology,

457
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:49,240
and the factory is still using the same machines today.

458
00:24:49,340 --> 00:24:52,500
We've had them since the first day the factory's been opened.

459
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:54,320
It's definitely a piece of history,

460
00:24:54,321 --> 00:24:58,240
and this thing has lasted us for 64 years now.

461
00:24:58,760 --> 00:25:01,100
And after almost six and a half decades,

462
00:25:01,300 --> 00:25:04,980
the foundations of fortune cookie making have not changed.

463
00:25:04,981 --> 00:25:07,480
This is the first thing we do every morning.

464
00:25:07,481 --> 00:25:13,720
We start by mixing flour, sugar, oil, water into this machine.

465
00:25:14,580 --> 00:25:17,340
My mother is the one who makes the recipe now.

466
00:25:17,460 --> 00:25:23,780
She's the only one who is skilled enough to make the cookies come out with the right crunch and consistency.

467
00:25:24,080 --> 00:25:31,080
So she'll add more or less water or more flour or more sugar, depending on the humidity, the temperature.

468
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:33,940
That changes on a day-to-day basis.

469
00:25:33,941 --> 00:25:37,400
She can detect those little differences and adjust accordingly.

470
00:25:38,900 --> 00:25:43,200
Once Alicia's mom is able to get the batter to its perfect consistency,

471
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:47,640
it's transported and poured into the Fortune cookie processing machine.

472
00:25:47,860 --> 00:25:52,780
The batter travels down a pump that drips it onto individual hot plates.

473
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:59,200
As it goes inside, the flames is going to cook the cookies for about three minutes.

474
00:25:59,201 --> 00:26:01,660
and when it comes out, it's gonna pop open,

475
00:26:01,661 --> 00:26:05,140
and the cookies are gonna be ready to take off.

476
00:26:05,141 --> 00:26:06,740
The merry-go-round of hot plates

477
00:26:06,741 --> 00:26:10,240
can crank out 5,000 cookies a day.

478
00:26:10,241 --> 00:26:12,440
But of course, it isn't a fortune cookie

479
00:26:12,441 --> 00:26:14,580
without the signature fortune inside.

480
00:26:14,581 --> 00:26:17,220
Every factory has their own source.

481
00:26:17,221 --> 00:26:20,520
Some of the previous factories employed teachers

482
00:26:20,521 --> 00:26:24,160
and freelance writers to write fortunes for them.

483
00:26:24,161 --> 00:26:28,520
In fact, fortune cookie writer is still a job to this day.

484
00:26:28,521 --> 00:26:30,920
But for Oakland Fortune Cookie Factory,

485
00:26:30,921 --> 00:26:33,460
the messages inside aren't outsourced.

486
00:26:33,461 --> 00:26:36,000
We try to come up with our own fortunes.

487
00:26:36,001 --> 00:26:39,500
We collect fortunes from stories, from news,

488
00:26:39,501 --> 00:26:43,000
from online articles, and have those made and cut

489
00:26:43,001 --> 00:26:45,840
into little fortune pieces.

490
00:26:45,841 --> 00:26:48,580
The fortune's actually inserted right

491
00:26:48,581 --> 00:26:50,000
as the cookie is folded.

492
00:26:52,580 --> 00:26:56,080
As these cookies come off the press,

493
00:26:56,081 --> 00:26:58,320
the cookies, for the first few seconds,

494
00:26:58,321 --> 00:27:01,160
is still very pliable and soft.

495
00:27:01,161 --> 00:27:05,300
So she has one chance to quickly take a fortune,

496
00:27:05,301 --> 00:27:07,620
put it into cookie, fold it up like a taco,

497
00:27:07,621 --> 00:27:10,160
and then bend it in half and let it harden

498
00:27:10,161 --> 00:27:12,100
into a fortune cookie shape.

499
00:27:12,101 --> 00:27:14,080
For most fortune cookie factories,

500
00:27:14,081 --> 00:27:16,240
this process has been mechanized,

501
00:27:16,241 --> 00:27:19,180
eliminating the unique skills and expertise

502
00:27:19,181 --> 00:27:21,000
that are on display here.

503
00:27:21,001 --> 00:27:23,680
This process is almost a lost art,

504
00:27:23,681 --> 00:27:26,920
because even if you know how to fold it,

505
00:27:26,921 --> 00:27:30,760
It's so incredibly difficult to do it at a very high speed

506
00:27:30,761 --> 00:27:32,540
and with the right precision.

507
00:27:32,541 --> 00:27:35,920
Miss Yi here can fold about 500 fortune cookies

508
00:27:35,921 --> 00:27:38,440
an hour on her own.

509
00:27:38,441 --> 00:27:41,420
That's not the only thing done differently here.

510
00:27:44,700 --> 00:27:47,360
In 2017, the Oakland Fortune Cookie

511
00:27:47,361 --> 00:27:51,180
Factory introduced beautifully designed, hand-dipped,

512
00:27:51,181 --> 00:27:54,720
and decorated fortune cookies that are custom-made

513
00:27:54,721 --> 00:27:58,060
and can feature whatever fortune you want.

514
00:27:58,061 --> 00:28:01,000
Every cookie we now make has a story

515
00:28:01,001 --> 00:28:03,520
or serve a specific purpose.

516
00:28:03,521 --> 00:28:05,420
We want to challenge what people think

517
00:28:05,421 --> 00:28:08,680
a fortune cookie can look like and taste like.

518
00:28:09,900 --> 00:28:11,440
Back to Albany.

519
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:15,620
Deep inside the Entenmann's facility,

520
00:28:15,621 --> 00:28:18,360
my first moment of truth is approaching.

521
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:21,540
This is where Kathleen says

522
00:28:21,541 --> 00:28:23,640
Entenmann's soft-baked chocolate chip cookies

523
00:28:23,641 --> 00:28:25,620
are at their best.

524
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:31,440
So this is the first section of the cooling tunnel

525
00:28:31,441 --> 00:28:33,740
before they get to the secondary cooler

526
00:28:33,741 --> 00:28:36,260
so that they're cool enough to pack.

527
00:28:36,261 --> 00:28:39,520
The cookies must spend about 45 minutes here

528
00:28:39,521 --> 00:28:41,400
before moving on to be boxed

529
00:28:41,401 --> 00:28:44,200
in that iconic see-through window box

530
00:28:44,201 --> 00:28:46,340
we all know and love.

531
00:28:46,341 --> 00:28:48,200
If not cooled properly,

532
00:28:48,201 --> 00:28:50,380
the cookies will lose their shape and moisture

533
00:28:50,381 --> 00:28:51,740
and they could break apart.

534
00:28:51,741 --> 00:28:55,340
No one gets to eat them before this cooling process.

535
00:28:55,341 --> 00:28:57,140
Well, except for me.

536
00:28:57,141 --> 00:28:59,480
So this is the best place to taste them?

537
00:28:59,481 --> 00:29:00,320
Right here.

538
00:29:00,321 --> 00:29:01,820
This is the exact spot.

539
00:29:01,821 --> 00:29:03,480
Well, this is your place.

540
00:29:03,481 --> 00:29:04,940
Milady, would you mind doing the honors?

541
00:29:04,941 --> 00:29:06,300
Yes, I will.

542
00:29:06,301 --> 00:29:08,280
This is an epic day.

543
00:29:08,281 --> 00:29:09,120
Yes.

544
00:29:11,780 --> 00:29:13,760
A cookie from the Cookie Oasis.

545
00:29:13,761 --> 00:29:16,140
Not too hot, not too crispy.

546
00:29:20,380 --> 00:29:22,380
Mmm.

547
00:29:22,381 --> 00:29:23,380
Mmm.

548
00:29:26,380 --> 00:29:28,380
That's like fudge inside.

549
00:29:28,381 --> 00:29:30,380
You take a tray of brownies out of the oven,

550
00:29:30,381 --> 00:29:34,380
and it's still chewy, but it's almost like a caramel.

551
00:29:36,380 --> 00:29:38,380
Oh.

552
00:29:38,381 --> 00:29:41,380
Sadly, I'll have to say goodbye to these delights

553
00:29:41,381 --> 00:29:43,380
as they make their way to be packaged

554
00:29:43,381 --> 00:29:47,380
in the iconic Entenmann's window box,

555
00:29:47,381 --> 00:29:50,840
box, which is where Kathleen is taking us now.

556
00:29:50,841 --> 00:29:54,900
But as we make our way over, I'm reminded of another cookie

557
00:29:54,901 --> 00:29:57,040
with an equally famous box.

558
00:29:57,041 --> 00:29:58,700
The stories of cookies in America

559
00:29:58,701 --> 00:30:01,320
wouldn't be complete without the Girl Scouts.

560
00:30:01,321 --> 00:30:03,780
Girl Scout cookies represent a nostalgia

561
00:30:03,781 --> 00:30:06,220
that make people so happy.

562
00:30:06,221 --> 00:30:08,740
Girl Scout season is once a year.

563
00:30:08,741 --> 00:30:13,080
I have never seen people get so excited over a cookie.

564
00:30:13,081 --> 00:30:15,820
Between January and April, every single year,

565
00:30:15,821 --> 00:30:19,360
the Girl Scouts sell 200 million boxes of cookies,

566
00:30:19,361 --> 00:30:22,460
which is half a box for every person living in America.

567
00:30:22,461 --> 00:30:24,460
But what most people don't know

568
00:30:24,461 --> 00:30:28,300
is the Girl Scouts have two different baking companies

569
00:30:28,301 --> 00:30:30,460
to turn out their annual cookies.

570
00:30:30,461 --> 00:30:33,180
For example, if you live in Dallas,

571
00:30:33,181 --> 00:30:36,380
you'll get Do-Si-Dos by Little Brownie Bakers.

572
00:30:36,381 --> 00:30:39,540
But if you go about 30 miles west to Fort Worth,

573
00:30:39,541 --> 00:30:42,620
ABC Bakers are making the same cookie,

574
00:30:42,621 --> 00:30:44,940
but calling them peanut butter sandwiches.

575
00:30:44,941 --> 00:30:47,980
If you're from Rhode Island, you have Caramel Delights.

576
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:50,120
Whereas if you're from Youngstown, Ohio,

577
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:52,680
you probably call them Samoas.

578
00:30:53,380 --> 00:30:55,460
Thin Mints are the only variety

579
00:30:55,461 --> 00:30:58,060
with the same name across both bakers.

580
00:30:58,280 --> 00:30:58,980
It's interesting.

581
00:30:58,981 --> 00:31:02,080
I think the Girl Scouts are an amazing marketing experience

582
00:31:02,081 --> 00:31:03,380
for these young women

583
00:31:03,381 --> 00:31:06,580
because it's all about financial awareness,

584
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:08,400
camaraderie, and collaboration.

585
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:10,340
And today in America,

586
00:31:10,341 --> 00:31:14,680
the Girl Scout cookie business is an $800 million business.

587
00:31:14,940 --> 00:31:20,100
And back at Entenmann's,

588
00:31:20,101 --> 00:31:23,620
these other national favorites are ready to be boxed.

589
00:31:24,160 --> 00:31:26,660
The iconic see-through window was introduced

590
00:31:26,661 --> 00:31:30,400
by William Entenmann Jr.'s wife and sons in 1959.

591
00:31:31,140 --> 00:31:32,380
Up until this point,

592
00:31:32,720 --> 00:31:36,020
baked goods came in white paper boxes tied with strings,

593
00:31:36,220 --> 00:31:38,940
so no one knew what they were getting inside.

594
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:42,200
But the Entenmann's family were pioneers,

595
00:31:42,201 --> 00:31:46,000
believing people were more inclined to buy what they could see.

596
00:31:46,001 --> 00:31:49,300
And boy, they won over a lot of customers,

597
00:31:49,301 --> 00:31:52,740
myself included, with that marketing angle.

598
00:31:55,740 --> 00:31:56,440
Wow.

599
00:31:56,441 --> 00:31:58,080
So they're famous for that window.

600
00:31:58,081 --> 00:31:58,820
That's intimate.

601
00:31:58,821 --> 00:32:00,980
Dude, this is like where a bill becomes a law.

602
00:32:00,981 --> 00:32:03,260
And this machine makes how many boxes?

603
00:32:03,261 --> 00:32:04,960
50 boxes a minute.

604
00:32:04,961 --> 00:32:09,260
At 50 boxes a minute, that's 3,000 boxes in an hour.

605
00:32:09,261 --> 00:32:13,260
And for a regular eight-hour workday and five-day workweek,

606
00:32:13,261 --> 00:32:18,260
that's 120,000 little boxed windows.

607
00:32:18,261 --> 00:32:21,260
So this machine is called a click clock.

608
00:32:21,261 --> 00:32:24,260
And as you can see, it goes clicked and it gets locked.

609
00:32:24,261 --> 00:32:28,260
I love that. And it folds it and assembles it all right here.

610
00:32:28,261 --> 00:32:29,260
Yep. I love that.

611
00:32:29,261 --> 00:32:32,260
So it goes from one of these to one of those,

612
00:32:32,261 --> 00:32:34,260
and then where are the cookies?

613
00:32:34,261 --> 00:32:40,660
So the cookies are going to be measured into the scale.

614
00:32:40,661 --> 00:32:41,720
Oh, my gosh.

615
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:43,500
I didn't even notice this whole conveyor belt.

616
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:49,880
High above the click lock machine is a long and winding conveyor belt transporting cookies

617
00:32:49,881 --> 00:32:52,740
from the cooling area to the boxing area.

618
00:32:53,420 --> 00:32:58,720
Next, the cookies travel to a scale that divvies them up by weight per box.

619
00:32:58,721 --> 00:33:02,400
each box must weigh around 340 grams,

620
00:33:02,860 --> 00:33:05,520
which is about 32 cookies per box.

621
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:08,220
Sometimes, to meet weight requirements,

622
00:33:08,580 --> 00:33:12,700
some lucky shoppers might find themselves with 33 cookies.

623
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:15,440
And I hope for that every day.

624
00:33:15,980 --> 00:33:18,420
Once the scale units reach the weight requirement,

625
00:33:18,860 --> 00:33:22,200
it drops the roughly 32 cookies into a little carriage,

626
00:33:22,600 --> 00:33:25,420
which then drops the cookies into the boxes.

627
00:33:25,421 --> 00:33:26,880
Oh, my God.

628
00:33:26,881 --> 00:33:28,960
This is really like Willy Wonka.

629
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:31,280
Can we see the final stage?

630
00:33:31,520 --> 00:33:32,220
Yes.

631
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:33,260
All right, let's do it.

632
00:33:37,020 --> 00:33:39,580
Before Entenmann's soft-baked chocolate chip cookies

633
00:33:39,581 --> 00:33:42,220
can be sent to the grocery store for you to pick up,

634
00:33:42,560 --> 00:33:45,780
the boxes will be sealed by this box-closing machine.

635
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:47,960
It places glue on the box,

636
00:33:48,400 --> 00:33:50,360
closes the flap to seal the box,

637
00:33:50,820 --> 00:33:53,180
marks it with the price and the date codes.

638
00:33:53,181 --> 00:34:01,640
Each box goes through one final weight check to make sure you're getting all 32 or maybe 33 cookies in each box.

639
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:05,480
Then a metal detector to ensure food safety.

640
00:34:05,920 --> 00:34:08,780
It's like an airport. These cookies have to go through a metal detector.

641
00:34:11,700 --> 00:34:17,620
And finally, at the end of the line, the boxes are packed by hand and ready to be shipped.

642
00:34:17,621 --> 00:34:19,620
There's been a machine for everything.

643
00:34:19,621 --> 00:34:23,460
But I love that the very last step before it reaches customers

644
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:24,620
is done by people.

645
00:34:24,621 --> 00:34:25,620
Yeah.

646
00:34:25,621 --> 00:34:26,620
From our hands to your table.

647
00:34:32,260 --> 00:34:35,620
With that many machines and that many people taking part

648
00:34:35,621 --> 00:34:39,620
in the baking process, how does Entenmann's maintain

649
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:42,620
the high standard of quality for which they're known?

650
00:34:42,621 --> 00:34:45,620
Well, that is a concern of this very department, quality,

651
00:34:45,621 --> 00:34:48,520
Quality control and this lovely lady to my left.

652
00:34:48,521 --> 00:34:50,820
Susan, thank you for having us.

653
00:34:50,821 --> 00:34:54,700
Can you explain how you do quality control

654
00:34:54,701 --> 00:34:56,440
on stuff like cookies and baked goods?

655
00:34:56,441 --> 00:34:57,360
Absolutely.

656
00:34:57,361 --> 00:34:59,060
First of all, we're going to weigh it.

657
00:34:59,061 --> 00:35:00,960
We have weight standards that we have to meet.

658
00:35:00,961 --> 00:35:01,800
OK.

659
00:35:01,801 --> 00:35:02,840
So it comes out to 342.

660
00:35:02,841 --> 00:35:04,180
Our label weight is 340.

661
00:35:04,181 --> 00:35:05,080
We're good to go.

662
00:35:05,081 --> 00:35:06,540
So what are we doing next?

663
00:35:06,541 --> 00:35:09,220
We're going to look for what the grain looks

664
00:35:09,221 --> 00:35:10,580
like on the inside of this cookie.

665
00:35:10,581 --> 00:35:16,580
See it looks a little doughy?

666
00:35:16,581 --> 00:35:17,580
Yeah.

667
00:35:17,581 --> 00:35:19,580
We call that the bone.

668
00:35:19,581 --> 00:35:22,580
Moist layer of dough right there.

669
00:35:22,581 --> 00:35:23,580
And this cookie has good bone.

670
00:35:23,581 --> 00:35:25,580
Who knew that cookies have bones?

671
00:35:25,581 --> 00:35:30,580
So that's sort of how you can tell if it's been over baked or under baked or something like that?

672
00:35:30,581 --> 00:35:31,580
Yes.

673
00:35:31,581 --> 00:35:34,580
Is that an industry standard term or an Entenmann standard term?

674
00:35:34,581 --> 00:35:35,580
Entenmann.

675
00:35:35,581 --> 00:35:38,580
Aside from weight and hunting for bones,

676
00:35:38,840 --> 00:35:42,560
there are so many quality control checks Susan will go through,

677
00:35:42,740 --> 00:35:45,260
like laying them out as though she were playing backgammon

678
00:35:45,261 --> 00:35:46,320
just to check the color

679
00:35:46,321 --> 00:35:49,140
and checking the size against a chart

680
00:35:49,141 --> 00:35:50,920
to make sure they're not too small.

681
00:35:51,380 --> 00:35:56,220
But what's next is what I think makes Susan's job a dream job.

682
00:35:56,980 --> 00:35:58,360
One last test.

683
00:35:59,520 --> 00:36:00,900
We get to eat them now.

684
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:02,860
Oh, yes.

685
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:04,660
Quality control is my favorite.

686
00:36:05,580 --> 00:36:10,320
Mmm, good bones.

687
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:11,820
Good cookie.

688
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:17,680
It evokes home baked without question.

689
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:22,580
So before I let you go, I'm just so, because this looks like a lab, this is sort of like

690
00:36:22,581 --> 00:36:23,620
cookie CSI.

691
00:36:23,820 --> 00:36:28,280
I'm looking over here and I see like little things that are for like food evaluation use,

692
00:36:28,340 --> 00:36:29,440
little crumbles and stuff.

693
00:36:29,760 --> 00:36:30,760
What is all this stuff?

694
00:36:32,380 --> 00:36:33,080
Inclusions.

695
00:36:33,640 --> 00:36:36,240
They're just little bits that you put into your batter or don't.

696
00:36:36,440 --> 00:36:37,780
A chocolate chip is an inclusion.

697
00:36:38,940 --> 00:36:40,200
Well, okay, maple.

698
00:36:40,860 --> 00:36:47,040
So is this maple something that is currently being worked on or that they're trying to include?

699
00:36:47,560 --> 00:36:48,840
I cannot say no.

700
00:36:51,340 --> 00:36:53,820
I thought I had a cool job. I was very wrong.

701
00:36:54,900 --> 00:36:56,040
Would you like to taste it?

702
00:36:56,680 --> 00:36:57,380
Can I?

703
00:36:57,381 --> 00:36:58,081
Okay.

704
00:37:02,080 --> 00:37:02,780
Mmm.

705
00:37:02,860 --> 00:37:03,700
Mmm.

706
00:37:03,701 --> 00:37:04,520
Mmm.

707
00:37:04,521 --> 00:37:08,900
Oh, it's like little crumbly bits of French toast

708
00:37:08,901 --> 00:37:10,260
and happiness.

709
00:37:10,261 --> 00:37:13,400
Oh, it's that good and super maple-y.

710
00:37:13,401 --> 00:37:16,900
All right, maple lovers, keep an eye out,

711
00:37:16,901 --> 00:37:21,020
because these little granules of joy are coming your way.

712
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:23,620
So what happens with the rest of these?

713
00:37:23,621 --> 00:37:24,820
They go in the garbage.

714
00:37:24,821 --> 00:37:25,820
Oh, the hell they do.

715
00:37:25,821 --> 00:37:26,820
May I take two more?

716
00:37:26,821 --> 00:37:27,820
Please.

717
00:37:30,220 --> 00:37:32,120
Oh, cookies.

718
00:37:32,121 --> 00:37:37,540
We eat more than 2 billion of them per year, and that is just as Americans.

719
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:42,040
Globally, the cookies industry is worth over $30 billion.

720
00:37:42,840 --> 00:37:44,840
And it is no wonder.

721
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:50,820
Cookies are made with so much love and magic by machines and humans,

722
00:37:50,821 --> 00:37:53,600
like the great ones we met at Otis Spunkmeyer,

723
00:37:53,920 --> 00:37:58,400
at the Oakland Fortune Cookie Factory, and right here at Entenmann's.

724
00:37:58,900 --> 00:38:01,260
Some cookies were inspired by experiments.

725
00:38:01,261 --> 00:38:04,260
Others stumbled upon by accident.

726
00:38:04,360 --> 00:38:07,860
Some cookies take us to far off lands

727
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:11,000
or keep us cozy right here at home.

728
00:38:11,100 --> 00:38:16,800
But one thing is for sure, cookies are marvelous.

